INT4 verify attention on tensor cores: int8 Q.K, bf16 P.V, one query block.

v1/attention/ops/int4_per_token_head.py converts the unpacked nibbles, the
query rows and the softmax probabilities to fp32 before tl.dot. On sm_89 an
fp32 tl.dot runs on TF32 tensor cores (10-bit mantissa), while the zero-point
correction next to it (Q_sum * k_zp, Pv_zp_sum) stays fp32, so the two terms
of each subtraction are rounded differently. BLOCK_M is 16 for GQA groups up
to 16; with 6 query heads per KV head that is BLOCK_Q = 2 query tokens per
block, so an 8-token verify batch launches 4 query blocks per KV head and each
of them streams the whole KV range.

This patch quantizes each query row to int8 (scale = absmax / 127, rounded)
after the RHT, takes K as int8 (the nibbles 0..15 are exact), computes Q.K as
an int32 dot and applies q_scale, k_scale and the zero-point term afterwards;
P and V go to the P.V dot as bf16 (V exact in bf16), accumulation stays fp32.
BLOCK_M becomes 64 (BLOCK_Q = 10, one block per request), TILE_SIZE_DECODE 64,
TILE_SIZE_PREFILL 128 (was 32), num_warps 8. The writer is unchanged.

Measured on an RTX 4080 SUPER, 100k context, 16 layers in one CUDA graph,
16 separate caches written by reshape_and_cache_int4, warm clock, median of 9:

  kernel                                   ms/step   GB/s   registers / spills
  bf16 split-KV verify (spec_decode_attn)  12.0-12.4   530
  int4 before                              32.1-33.1    53   168 / 0    (4 warps)
  int4 after                               11.3-11.5   147   255 / 16   (8 warps)

  bytes per step: bf16 6.55 GB, int4 1.69 GB

Against the same kernel with input_precision="ieee" on all four dots (fp32
FMA, no TF32), same quantized inputs, random N(0,1) K/V, one layer, 100k:

